<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nantes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Condevicnum, Portus Namnetum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nantes is a city of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, about 50 km from the Atlantic. Beginning as the Gallo-Roman port of Condevicnum, or Portus Namnetum, of the Namnetes, it became a bishopric and a seat of the dukes of Brittany. In 1598 Henry IV issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes">Edict of Nantes</a> there, and in the 18th century it was France's leading slave-trading port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nantes.jpg" alt="Nantes" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Brosselin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_depuis_Butte_Sainte-Anne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brittany, the Armorican peninsula settled by migrants from south-western Britain in the fifth and sixth centuries, had formed a kingdom under Nominoe, Erispoe and Salomon in the ninth century, when its rulers fought the Carolingians and won the counties of Rennes and <b>Nantes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century Bordeaux and <b>Nantes</b> grew rich on Atlantic trade in sugar, coffee and enslaved Africans, centred on the colony of Saint-Domingue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Fortunes came from overseas trade through Bordeaux and <b>Nantes</b>, from lending to the crown and from collecting its taxes, and the bourgeois put them into land: by 1789 they owned about a third of France’s arable land.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Angers that followed, Charles recognised Erispoe as ruler of the Bretons, with Rennes and <b>Nantes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-22">22 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Waroch repeatedly raided Frankish territory around Rennes and <b>Nantes</b> and defeated or bought off Frankish armies sent by Guntram and Chilperic I in the 570s and 580s.</blockquote><a href="/state/broerec">Broërec</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Erispoe won again at Jengland in 851, and the Treaty of Angers recognised him as king with Rennes, <b>Nantes</b> and the Pays de Retz.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brittany">Kingdom of Brittany</a></li></ul></section>
